#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "7fc6630d-00e2-5528-a1d5-4c7f7b43e8a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-562" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) = 4t^2 - 3t^3,$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the velocity (in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s$) of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{4}{3}(0)^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = \\dfrac{4}{3}t^3 -\\dfrac{3}{4}t^4 + 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082930 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20159e8-8f86-5bf8-b87e-3eb990b819f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Not a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "9a655242-0547-5a0a-b4cc-2fb3caa11584" :question/math-academy-id "e-563" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) =2e^{-t}+6t^2$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-4 \\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the speed of the particle at the moment  $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{-t}+6{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(-{e}^{-t})+\\frac{6}{3}{t}^{3}+C \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-t}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0) = -4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-2{e}^{-0}+2\\cdot {0}^{3}+C \\\\ -4 & =-2+0+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = -2  e^{-t}+ 2t^3 -2.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =-2{e}^{-1}+2(1)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}+2-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{e}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\dfrac 2 e\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c38d0bd-2192-5e9b-b150-07a5086649a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289897} {:answer/id #uuid "74e83db5-8638-5454-8e90-b5e4296bc9a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289898} {:answer/id #uuid "56e7dee2-4197-57f1-8ebc-024d6d715bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289899} {:answer/id #uuid "93740cc5-d830-5826-b8d3-8383a5ef3da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289900} {:answer/id #uuid "b25f5106-8bb7-5827-85fb-54638328c9a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56e7dee2-4197-57f1-8ebc-024d6d715bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289899} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aabe25f-0df0-5c75-a151-4158c6a44722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbd286de-d18c-5c8c-8baf-fe50f2bdfc52" :question/math-academy-id "q-102693" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3+8t-3{t}^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3+8t-3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3t+4{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=-1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =3(0)+4(0)^{2}-(0)^{3}+C \\\\ -1 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3t+4{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}-1.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =3(2)+4(2)^{2}-(2)^{3}-1 \\\\  & =6+16-8-1 \\\\  & =13\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $13\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59e9c8d6-7ff2-5e42-9e17-de3f02113652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^6+t^5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293097} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5fd701-8fb5-5d48-a243-6eb8071712ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^6+t^5+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec5fd701-8fb5-5d48-a243-6eb8071712ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^6+t^5+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293098} :answer-field/id #uuid "db3334eb-9035-531f-a680-f558d38afd21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd69d235-be6f-5d7e-afe1-9f5f4f1fa348" :question/math-academy-id "q-346883" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=12{t}^{5}+5{t}^{4},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=6\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (12{t}^{5}+5{t}^{4})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{6}+{t}^{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=6.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2({1}^{6})+{1}^{5}+C \\\\ 6 & =3+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{6}+{t}^{5}+3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20159e8-8f86-5bf8-b87e-3eb990b819f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Not a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7f577e6-0388-59af-975d-ed57b6e34411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7f577e6-0388-59af-975d-ed57b6e34411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293104} :answer-field/id #uuid "139dc4dd-2b62-56d0-8df5-e7a62b035a69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "842fcf81-235b-5826-abfd-5c8a902285e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-351058" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=5{e}^{t},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 5{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =5{e}^{0}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =5+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=5{e}^{t}-\\frac{9}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =5{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =5(2)-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1d09c6d-435a-5c1c-ae62-7a96e9239ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10.8m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082377} {:answer/id #uuid "0b6d9665-5815-5ba5-8903-447745671dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16.8m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082378} {:answer/id #uuid "4a71d8be-dc17-5d42-aa3c-d72ebbb5d1b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.2m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082379} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ef86b-c513-597a-a36b-ff1f600f2249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.8m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082380} {:answer/id #uuid "715355f9-ee6a-570c-8cd2-6867a36b0167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.8m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ef86b-c513-597a-a36b-ff1f600f2249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.8m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082380} :answer-field/id #uuid "652e1e66-ecab-5d39-8f9a-a83b027d6d48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082376}] :question/id #uuid "2b990338-8621-58e9-88f4-f6c4e5a99132" :question/math-academy-id "q-7194" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $m/s^{2}$, given by the function $a(t) = \\frac{1}{2}t^{4} - 3t^{3} + t$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v =-10m/s$ when $t = 0s$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t = 2s$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e65870-2b27-5894-a0a0-203908584a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289928} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd80d09-4e56-593c-93b9-b51f7f9c1c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289929} {:answer/id #uuid "71da9d86-9793-50ad-bd48-53f7e54f5912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289930} {:answer/id #uuid "64ea1d6b-87e4-549e-8533-49aa0fdf4c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289931} {:answer/id #uuid "8e305e54-abfc-5bff-96e1-5a8ab1c1d05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71da9d86-9793-50ad-bd48-53f7e54f5912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289930} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0556b96-5033-5370-8905-0acdcd83681b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f8fadaa-0d78-521b-ae7c-e7c1f771ff78" :question/math-academy-id "q-81474" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=6{t}^{2}-2t-10$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6{t}^{2}-2t-10)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-10t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=-4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}-10(1)+C \\\\ -4 & =2-1-10+C \\\\ C & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-10t+5.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=3\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =2(3)^{3}-(3)^{2}-10(3)+5 \\\\  & =54-9-30+5 \\\\  & =20\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $20\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}]}
